From 43a48996be9e22b377e2e4b90d0ec0bc64c2390f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 02:28:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Run the CSD arms from Python, on a simulation proven
 identical to the .m one

The CSD half of the manuscript simulation split onto a Python kernel, so it
runs in minutes instead of waiting on SMI.fit, and exports its results for the
.m side to draw figures from.

deconv_comparison/smi_sim.py is the forward model in Python: the protocol
reader, SMI.get_even_SH, the Watson fODF and its projection onto plm, K_l(b),
the forward convolution, Rician noise, and an MRtrix .mih writer. Every one of
those mirrors a named function in SMI.m or helpers/, and none of it is trusted
because it looks right.

Identity with smi_manuscript_60deg.m is MEASURED. dump_reference.m writes the
Octave side of the forward model, check_python_vs_octave.py recomputes it in
Python, and Step 0 of the notebook runs that comparison and fails loudly if it
does not pass. 22 arrays compared; the noise-free signal agrees to 6.7e-15.

Two rows are compared relatively rather than absolutely, both for reasons worth
recording: Octave's textscan and Python's float() round decimal strings like
"2.99" differently in the last bit (80 of 288 b values, 1.5e-16 relative), and
a Watson at kappa 16 peaks at exp(16) ~ 9e6, where an absolute tolerance is a
demand for 22 significant digits.

The noise realisations are deliberately NOT identical, and pretending otherwise
would be the kind of assumption this package exists to avoid: Octave's legacy
randn and numpy's PCG64 cannot be reconciled without reimplementing one inside
the other. So the noisy signal is exported instead. Fitting SMI to C.signal
rather than to a fresh draw is what puts all three arms on common random
numbers.

The response is dispersion matched rather than the delta response every other
arm here gets:

    r_l(b) = K_l(b) * p_l^Watson(kappa) * sqrt((2l+1) * 4*pi)

A fibre in this simulation is a Watson at kappa 16, not a delta, so this is the
response a perfect dwi2response would recover. Measured at b = 3 it lands
between the delta and the three estimators and accounts for about 69% of the
l = 2 gap between them, which is direct support for section 6.3's attribution
of response bluntness to fibre dispersion. At l = 4 and 6 it overshoots
slightly, so kappa 16 disperses a little more than the phantom those estimators
were run on.

A finding rather than a failure, called out in the notebook and the README:
MSMT-CSD's noise-free angular error is 7.80 deg against a 1.27 deg ceiling with
the dispersion-matched response, and 2.82 deg with the delta. It finds both
lobes either way -- the count is right -- but its primary lobe is displaced,
and the displacement roughly triples when the response is blunted. That
reproduces from a third direction the report's finding that MSMT's 60 degree
error is response-limited. SSST-CSD sits exactly on the ceiling in both cases.

The round trip back into Octave is checked, not assumed. read_csd_export.m
reads the export; test_csd_roundtrip.m re-scores the exported SH coefficients
with the Octave peak finder and compares against what Python printed -- peak
counts and spurious counts reproduce exactly, mean angular error to 4.6e-10
deg. helpers/fODF_peak_score.m is that peak finder, extracted from Step 7 of
smi_manuscript_60deg.m so the two languages can be shown to agree rather than
merely to look similar. figures_csd_arms.m draws the figures from the export.

Two documentation corrections fall out of this:

* Octave's print is NOT broken in this container. figures_csd_arms.m writes all
  three of its PNGs without error, and the notebook's matplotlib figures render
  inline. The handoff said otherwise and used it to excuse never rendering a
  figure; gnuplot still ignores camlight and lighting, so glyphs are flat
  shaded, but they render.
* notebooks/README.md contradicted itself, claiming in one section that the CSD
  arms appear in the figures automatically and in another that they cannot.
  The stale paragraph now points at the two notebooks that actually run them.

